findings.recommendation:
Replace the in-test recomputation with a hard-coded expected hex literal computed once (e.g. `echo -n 'test-webhook-secret-deadbeef' | shasum -a 256`) and inline as a String constant. Example:
  String input = "test-webhook-secret-deadbeef";
  String expected = "<paste 64-char lowercase hex from sha256sum>";
  assertThat(Sha256Hex.hex(input)).isEqualTo(expected);
This turns the test into a true parity guard against algorithm/encoding drift.

Add one focused test using a Logback ListAppender attached to the WebhookSecretVerifier logger (or the package logger), invoke verify() with a recognizable plaintext + hash, and assert that no log event's formatted message contains the plaintext, the storedHashHex, or the candidate hash. Example skeleton:
  Logger log = (Logger) LoggerFactory.getLogger(WebhookSecretVerifier.class);
  ListAppender<ILoggingEvent> appender = new ListAppender<>(); appender.start(); log.addAppender(appender);
  verifier.verify("sentinel-PLAINTEXT-xyz", Sha256Hex.hex("sentinel-PLAINTEXT-xyz"));
  assertThat(appender.list).allSatisfy(e -> assertThat(e.getFormattedMessage()).doesNotContain("sentinel-PLAINTEXT-xyz"));
This turns a documented invariant into a regression guard.
